From cf8778ea55e64760941d991b352de7f87f18ee9d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HuskerMinion <28571744+HuskerMinion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 25/40] mmc: dw_mmc-rtd129x: Add the Realtek RTD129x eMMC glue

The eMMC controller of the RTD1293/RTD1295/RTD1296 is a DesignWare MSHC
(register file, FIFO at 0x200, 32-bit IDMAC) behind a Realtek wrapper
above 0x400. The vendor driver (rtkemmc) reimplements the whole host on
top of these registers; this glue uses dw_mmc instead and keeps only
what the vendor driver does differently.

Register access:
- Every register access takes the RTD129x RBUS erratum lock, and every
  write is preceded by an SB2 bus sync, like the vendor sync(). CMDARG
  and CLKDIV are re-written until they read back, as in the vendor
  code.
- The register values follow the vendor driver: no IDMAC fixed bursts,
  no CTRL.dma_enable (vendor CTRL 0x02000010), the vendor FIFOTH (MSIZE
  1, RX watermark 1, TX watermark 127), and the 512-byte card read
  threshold always enabled. dw_mmc disables the threshold below HS200,
  so at 50 MHz a block read could start without room in the FIFO.
- HCON reports a 64-bit host data width; if dw_mmc falls back to PIO,
  32-bit FIFO accesses are used.

IDMAC:
This IDMAC does not write descriptors back, so the OWN bit of every
descriptor it used stays set after a transfer. dw_mmc's
dw_mci_prepare_desc32() polls OWN for up to 100 ms before it reuses a
descriptor, then re-initializes the ring and moves the transfer by PIO.
The vendor driver never reads its descriptors back and rewrites them
for every transfer (make_sg_des()). Do the same: replace dw_mmc's IDMAC
start with one that builds the chain from scratch for every request
(32-bit chained descriptors of at most 4 KiB) and never polls OWN, and
clear OWN on the used chain when the transfer has completed, and before
a data request while the host is idle, which also covers the error and
stop paths.

The chain is started with the vendor sequence: CH and DIC also on the
last descriptor, IDMAC interrupts off, DBADDR rewritten before each
start, no per-request DMA reset and no poll demand (the data command
starts the IDMAC). dw_mmc's own start sequence works for single
descriptor requests, but froze the SoC as soon as a request spanned
several descriptors.

Completion and recovery:
- Without IDMAC interrupts, a data request completes as in the vendor
  driver: after DATA_OVER, once the wrapper DMA_DONE flag is set (reads)
  and TBBCNT has reached BYTCNT. A work item checks this every 4 ms
  while a data request is in flight. The dma_complete hook then waits
  briefly for DMA_DONE after a read and clears it for the next transfer.
  DMA_DONE is also cleared before each IDMAC start, since the error and
  stop paths can leave it set, so it always belongs to the current
  transfer.
- The same work item looks at MINTSTS and IDSTS and runs the dw_mmc
  interrupt handler through dw_mci_handle_pending_irq() if status is
  pending that was never delivered, as the vendor driver polls.
- dw_mmc has no timeout for a data transfer whose DATA_OVER arrived but
  whose DMA did not finish, and a hang there held the boot forever in
  the partition scan. When the DMA is still not done 2 s after the
  request started (and at least 100 ms after DATA_OVER), the work item
  dumps the registers, stops the DMA and fails the data with
  -ETIMEDOUT.
- A request that sees no DATA_OVER at all is failed the same way, like
  dw_mmc's own data timeout timer, but only after a limit derived from
  the card's data timeout times the block count plus 2 s, at least 2 s
  and at most the 60 s block request timeout, so that large writes to a
  slow but in-spec card are not cut short.
The controller interrupt is disabled while the work item looks at the
state, so a request is never completed twice. The tracked request is
dropped once it has finished, so a later request that reuses its memory
is never mistaken for it, and at remove, after the host is gone, before
the work item is canceled.

Clocks and pads:
- At probe the eMMC PLL is set to 100 MHz (N code 0x57) with sample and
  drive phase 0, and the wrapper clock generator is programmed as in
  the vendor SDR fallback. With dw_mmc's divider that gives 400 kHz for
  identification and 50 MHz high-speed SDR. HS200, HS400 and DDR52 are
  not supported yet, so the HS200/HS400 state that U-Boot leaves behind
  is cleared. Chips before revision A01 are not supported.
- The pad drive is the vendor init value 0x33 up to 26 MHz and the
  realtek,drive-hs codes (default: the vendor SDR50 pddrive_nf_s0
  values) above. EMMC_PAD_CTL is left as U-Boot set it, the 1.8 V
  signaling bit is set, and there is no voltage switch.
- The pads are left as U-Boot muxed them; there is no pinctrl driver.

Write protection:
For bring-up on boards that boot from the eMMC, "realtek,read-only"
makes the driver hide the write, erase, write-protect and lock command
classes in the CSD, so that the MMC block driver marks every area
read-only, and refuse write, erase and write-protect commands and
EXT_CSD writes outside a short list needed for card init and partition
switching (which may never change the boot configuration). The register
accessors also refuse to start a data write or an erase command as a
last line of defense.

The read-only module parameter allow_writes=1 lifts all of this. With
writes enabled, data writes and erases pass in the user area only, and
write_min_lba refuses them below a block address, so that a test can
protect the partitions in front of it. Still refused are EXT_CSD
writes outside the list, CMD26/27 (program CID/CSD), CMD28/29 (write
protect), CMD42 (lock), the vendor commands CMD60-63, and every
CMD24/CMD25 data write and erase while a boot or general purpose
partition is selected. With the RPMB partition selected, writes and
erases are always refused, which covers the irreversible RPMB key
programming and makes RPMB unusable on these boards.

boot1_writes allows plain CMD24/CMD25 data writes while the boot1
hardware partition is selected, in read-only and in write mode, for
write tests through MMC_IOC_CMD. In read-only mode the user area and
boot0 stay refused, and the CSD patch keeps every block device
read-only.

CMD5 (sleep) and CMD15 (go inactive) are always refused: these boards
cannot power-cycle the eMMC, and the boot loader must find the card
awake after a warm reboot.

A sysfs file, rtd129x_status, reports the read-only state and counters
of the completion and recovery paths.
---
 drivers/mmc/host/Kconfig          |   29 +
 drivers/mmc/host/Makefile         |    1 +
 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rtd129x.c | 1720 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 1750 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-rtd129x.c

